  "scenario": "A rural buyer shops only every couple of months and wants a chocolate-and-nut pantry stash that remains genuinely enjoyable through week ten rather than merely edible. A previous batch of chocolate developed a chalky white coating and nut snacks tasted stale. The frozen packet contains five exact seller offers: an 8-count 3.6-ounce Hershey snack-size pack described as individually wrapped but whose page also reports 1.41 ounces in Product Dimensions; a 5-pound West End Foods bundle of Ghirardelli 60-percent squares; a 14-ounce resealable bag of Blue Diamond dark-chocolate-flavored almonds; twelve 4.5-ounce resealable pouches of Second Nature dark-chocolate nut mix whose page warns that warm weather can affect chocolate; and a selected 2-pound Oh Nuts almond offer whose page also reports a conflicting 1.2-pound Package Dimensions weight. General evidence distinguishes fat bloom from sugar bloom, explains tempering and cocoa-butter crystal forms, links oxygen-driven autoxidation to rancidity, limits MVTR and OTR comparisons to declared test conditions and complete package structures, and limits discrimination testing to detectable differences rather than preference magnitude. Four community discussions provide only scoped operational observations about pocket melting, opening and clipping snack bags, labeling stored containers, and historical local block-chocolate sourcing. No captured source verifies an exact lot, best-before date, complete package construction, measured barrier rate, ten-week storage history, or matched week-ten sensory result for any offer.",
